// Vitest Snapshot v1, https://vitest.dev/guide/snapshot.html

exports[`the offline demo > produces exactly this output 1`] = `
"
jml demo: a joiner/mover/leaver lifecycle with no credentials and no network.

HR system      the shipped demo fixture (7 people, 3 employed)
people store   in memory, empty at the start
providers      the shipped fakes; every call is recorded, nothing leaves this process
clock          pinned to 2026-01-15, then moved forward
one laptop     sys-demo-laptop bound to Robin Ellis, to show the deletion gate

The fixture explains what each person is there to demonstrate:
  The demo company. Seven people, each one there to make a different rule visible.
  Every date is written around demoToday, so pin the clock to that date when replaying it.
  
  p-1001  employed, nothing to do. Also the manager the leavers report to, so the
          Drive transfer has somewhere to go.
  p-1002  starts after demoToday, so the sync derives 'hired' and no access work runs.
  p-1003  left on demoToday. This is the day-0 case: suspend, auto-reply, licence.
  p-1004  left six days before demoToday, so the transfer phase is due. In a demo the
          store already holds suspendedAt for this person; the HR system only ever
          shows the leaving date.
  p-1005  left three days before demoToday and was renamed on the way out, so the HR
          system now shows a plus-addressed mailbox. This is the person a role-change
          branch once treated as brand new, which let them inherit a colleague's
          account ids. The id is unchanged, so the sync must treat the new address as
          an alias of the same person.
  p-1006  left long before the lookback window. Parked for a human, never offboarded
          automatically: a historic leaver is what a pruned tombstone looks like.
  p-1007  employed, no work mailbox. Stays in the snapshot with an empty address,
          because removing them here would look exactly like somebody leaving.

=== The day before anybody left  (2026-01-14) ===
The HR system says everybody is employed. The sync creates a row for each of them,
and derives hired rather than active for anybody whose start date is still ahead.
Nothing else happens: there is nobody to offboard.


